{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WkjEytZjUu6RDarVRucKi9","type":"tool_result","content":"R02.3 Credential limits, tool permissions, and B05 action gates block the actions an injected instruction could cause, enforced in code the model cannot change. \n\nE06.2 The gateway validates its own incoming token audience, issuer, expiry and scopes, and obtains separately authorized, backend-specific credentials while preserving agent and delegated-subject identity. \n\nE06.3 Every direct, dynamic, CLI/code-mode and wrapper call receives action/resource/tenant/argument authorization, request/response validation, rate limits and decision logging. \n\nB04.3 Later calls are blocked even with cached tokens, self-contained tokens, and open sessions: token lifetime is shorter than the revocation limit, or revocation is pushed to each service (for example CAEP) or checked on every call. \n\nE09.2 Stopping revokes descendants' credentials or leases, and leases or tokens are short-lived, so a disconnected worker loses authority within the stop time limit. \n\nA05.3 A containment control can disable a single instance or a single agent type (for example revoke its principal or scale it to zero) without touching the launching user's credentials. \n\nE03.6 Tool quarantine, bounded config propagation, recorded execution config versions, and rollback preserve revocations. \n\nA02.2 All six fields are written on denied, failed, async, and background actions too, not only on the successful main request path. \n\nB04.2 Revocation is applied at a trusted enforcement point (identity provider, gateway, or target service) and does not need the agent's cooperation. \n\nE09.1 A stop signal from the root reaches every descendant: nested children, remote workers, queued work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks. \n\nR11.1 A stop control halts new model calls and new tool calls when set. \n\nB01.2 Tools discovered from MCP servers are filtered against that allowlist before the model can see or call them. \n\nE03.1 New tools start disabled, an owner approves versioned definitions and grants before enablement, and approved descriptions/schemas have stored fingerprints. \n\nE04.1 A scanner runs on server instructions, tool names, titles, descriptions, input and output schemas, annotations, and prompt and resource templates before the model sees them. \n\nA07.4 A checker timeout, error, invalid output, or version mismatch makes the control that depends on it fail (block or escalate), not pass by default. \n\nC03.5 A mismatch blocks or quarantines the deployment, not only logs it. \n\nC04.3 Detected drift triggers an alert and a set response (block, quarantine, or rollback) defined in config, with a time limit. \n\nB05.1 High-impact operations are listed in code or config and blocked by default, including deletes, destructive updates, force-pushes, infrastructure teardown, payment changes, outbound sends, and deleting audit records or changing their retention. \n\nR12.1 For each operation type, code or config states whether it can be cancelled, completed, or only reconciled after a stop. \n\nC06.2 The suite includes cases for destructive-action denial, access limits, injection containment, audit attribution, and stopping, plus retrieval, memory, and checker tests where these apply. \n\nR02.5 Injection tests repeat each fixture a set number of times, include adaptive variants and schema-valid payloads, and report success rates. \n\nE05.1 Tools are keyed by verified server identity (endpoint, credential, or registry namespace) plus tool name, never by bare tool name or a server's self-reported name. \n\nE05.2 The registry flags duplicate and lookalike tool names across servers. \n\nE05.3 Approval is bound to the verified server identity, so a new server with a matching tool name or self-reported name does not inherit approval or replace the approved tool. \n\nE05 gap [('E05.1', 'no', 'app/mcp_client.py:100'), ('E05.2